---
import PageLayout from '../../layouts/PageLayout.astro';
import VGISubNav from '../../components/sections/VGISubNav.astro';
import PageHead from '../../components/ui/PageHead.astro';
import Button from '../../components/ui/Button.astro';
import CodeBlock from '../../components/ui/CodeBlock.astro';
import AgentQuickstart from '../../components/vgi/AgentQuickstart.astro';
import RecordedAgentBuild from '../../components/vgi/RecordedAgentBuild.astro';
import WorkerQueryFlow from '../../components/vgi/WorkerQueryFlow.astro';
import ShippingStory from '../../components/vgi/ShippingStory.astro';
import AgentBuildLoop from '../../components/vgi/AgentBuildLoop.astro';
import AgentLanguageMarks from '../../components/vgi/AgentLanguageMarks.astro';
import VgiWalkthroughVideo from '../../components/vgi/VgiWalkthroughVideo.astro';
import { VGI_SKILLS_REPO, vgiSkillsFile } from '../../lib/vgi-agents';
import { organization } from '../../lib/organization';
const description = 'See how a VGI worker brings your code into DuckDB. Follow a shipping-margin example, then let your coding agent build and test a worker in your chosen language.';
const jsonLd = [{ '@context': 'https://schema.org', '@type': 'TechArticle', headline: 'Build a VGI worker with your coding agent', description, url: 'https://query.farm/vgi/build-with-an-agent', author: organization }, { '@context': 'https://schema.org', '@type': 'BreadcrumbList', itemListElement: [{ '@type': 'ListItem', position: 1, name: 'VGI', item: 'https://query.farm/vgi/' }, { '@type': 'ListItem', position: 2, name: 'Build with an Agent', item: 'https://query.farm/vgi/build-with-an-agent' }] }];
---
<PageLayout title="Build a VGI worker with your coding agent" description={description} jsonLd={jsonLd}>
  <VGISubNav />
  <PageHead variant="section" eyebrow="VGI for coding agents" title="Give DuckDB the code your query is missing." hairband>
    <Fragment slot="lede">Your data is in DuckDB. But the function you need may live in a library, an API, or your application. A VGI worker makes that code callable from SQL. Your coding agent can build the worker and test the query.</Fragment>
    <div class="mt-6 flex flex-wrap gap-3"><Button href="#shipping-story" variant="primary" size="md">Follow a working example →</Button><Button href="#start" variant="secondary" size="md">Build your own</Button></div>
    <AgentLanguageMarks />
  </PageHead>

  <section class="section-container py-12 sm:py-16" id="why">
    <div class="max-w-[75ch]"><p class="qf-eyebrow text-sun-700">What we’re building, and why</p><h2 class="mt-3 text-3xl text-soil-900">The order table is only half the answer.</h2><p class="mt-4 leading-relaxed text-soil-700">Suppose you want to find orders that lose money after shipping. DuckDB already has each order’s revenue, product cost, weight, and destination. <strong class="text-soil-900">But the shipping rule belongs to your application.</strong></p><p class="mt-4 leading-relaxed text-soil-700">Therefore, expose that rule through a <strong class="text-soil-900">VGI worker</strong>: a normal program that runs your code and describes its functions to DuckDB. The VGI extension connects the two. Your query can call the shipping function and use its answer alongside the order data.</p></div>
    <WorkerQueryFlow />
    <p class="max-w-[75ch] text-sm leading-relaxed text-soil-700">VGI stands for <strong>Vector Gateway Interface</strong>. It passes typed columns using Apache Arrow, so a worker can process batches of rows. Workers can also expose tables and catalogs—for example, an API you want to join with local data.</p>
    <div class="mt-6"><p class="text-sm font-semibold text-soil-900">Write the worker where your libraries already live.</p><AgentLanguageMarks kind="languages" /></div>
  </section>

  <section class="section-container pb-14 sm:pb-20"><ShippingStory /></section>

  <section class="section-container pb-14 sm:pb-20" id="agent-role">
    <div class="max-w-[75ch]"><p class="qf-eyebrow text-sun-700">Why a coding agent helps</p><h2 class="mt-3 text-3xl text-soil-900">Give it a result it can prove.</h2><p class="mt-4 leading-relaxed text-soil-700">The desired result is specific: the right shipping quotes and the right orders in the report. That gives your agent a concrete job—implement the function, expose it through VGI, and check the actual SQL results.</p><p class="mt-4 leading-relaxed text-soil-700"><strong class="text-soil-900">But plausible source code is not enough.</strong> A worker can compile and still mishandle NULL, declare the wrong type, or fail on a second batch. The VGI skill supplies SDK guidance and a verifier, so the agent can find those failures, fix them, and leave tests you can rerun.</p></div>
    <AgentBuildLoop />
  </section>

  <section class="section-container pb-14 sm:pb-20" aria-label="Build your worker">
    <AgentQuickstart />
    <p class="mt-5 max-w-[90ch] text-sm leading-relaxed text-soil-700"><strong class="text-soil-900">Before you start:</strong> have your coding agent, your chosen language runtime, and <a href="https://docs.astral.sh/uv/" class="text-link underline">uv</a> available. The skill installer uses Node.js. The SQL verifier uses stock DuckDB; the worker stays in the language you choose. Start locally, then choose a <a href="/vgi/distributing-a-worker" class="text-link underline">distribution method</a> when the tests pass.</p>
  </section>

  <section class="section-container pb-14 sm:pb-20" id="watch"><VgiWalkthroughVideo /></section>

  <section class="section-container pb-14 sm:pb-20" id="proof">
    <div class="max-w-[75ch]"><h2 class="text-3xl text-soil-900">Start small. Keep the tests as it grows.</h2><p class="mt-4 leading-relaxed text-soil-700">Our shipping example uses fictional rates so you can inspect every result. Replace that one function with your own library, add another destination, or wrap an API. The same SQL assertions help you see what changed.</p><p class="mt-4 leading-relaxed text-soil-700">We also ran fresh Codex and Claude Code sessions on a smaller slugification task and checked their generated workers independently. Some finished within five minutes; one took longer. Those timings belong to that small task, not the shipping example or every project.</p></div>
    <details class="mt-7 rounded-xl border border-soil-300 p-5 sm:p-6" data-build-evidence><summary class="cursor-pointer font-semibold text-soil-900">Inspect the recorded agent builds, source, and timings</summary><div class="mt-8"><RecordedAgentBuild /></div></details>
    <p class="mt-5 text-sm leading-relaxed text-soil-700">We also gave the shipping request to a fresh Claude Code session. It built its worker in 2m 48s; independent verification passed the report, missing-rate, and multi-batch checks. <a href={vgiSkillsFile('evals/RESULTS.md')} class="text-link underline underline-offset-2">Read that evaluation →</a></p>
    <p class="mt-4 text-sm leading-relaxed text-soil-700">Add <code>vgi-test</code> to diagnose an existing worker or <code>vgi-package</code> to prepare and verify a local archive. <a href={vgiSkillsFile('README.md')} class="text-link underline underline-offset-2">Explore all three skills →</a></p>
  </section>

  <section class="section-container pb-16" id="engine">
    <details class="rounded-xl border border-soil-300 p-5 sm:p-6"><summary class="cursor-pointer font-semibold text-soil-900">The DuckDB setup your agent uses</summary>
      <div class="mt-4 min-w-0 text-sm leading-relaxed text-soil-700"><p>The quickstart uses stock DuckDB 2.0.0 and VGI from the signed <a href="/blog/duckdb-2-query-farm-repository/" class="text-link underline">Query Farm extension repository</a>:</p><div class="mt-4"><CodeBlock code={`SET allow_extension_repositories = 'allowed';\nCREATE EXTENSION REPOSITORY IF NOT EXISTS query_farm\n    WITH PREFIX 'https://duckdb-extensions.query.farm';\nINSTALL vgi FROM query_farm;\nLOAD vgi FROM query_farm;`} lang="sql" tryable={false} /></div><p class="mt-3">In later sessions, use <code>LOAD vgi FROM query_farm</code>. The recorded examples run on macOS ARM64, with Python and TypeScript examples also checked in Linux CI. Check the available build for your engine version and platform.</p><p class="mt-3"><a href="/products/haybarn" class="text-link underline">Haybarn</a> remains supported with <code>--engine haybarn</code>. You keep the engine of an existing project.</p></div>
    </details>
    <div class="mt-10 flex flex-wrap items-center gap-5"><Button href="#start" variant="primary">Choose your language and build →</Button><a href="/blog/build-vgi-workers-with-coding-agents/" class="text-sm text-link underline underline-offset-2">Read the full story</a><a href={VGI_SKILLS_REPO} class="text-sm text-link underline underline-offset-2">Skills and runnable examples</a></div>
  </section>
</PageLayout>
